<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Clisson</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Clisson is a town in the Loire-Atlantique <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, on the Sèvre Nantaise south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a>. Its castle, seat of the powerful Clisson family whose best-known member was the constable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olivier-de-clisson">Olivier de Clisson</a>, guarded the frontier between Brittany and Poitou. Destroyed in the Vendée war in 1794, the town was rebuilt in an Italian style in the early nineteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/clisson.jpg" alt="Clisson" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stephanemartin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eglise_notre_dame_clisson.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
